#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "a66e0264-85ff-5ded-af5d-dc17a9278226" :question/math-academy-id "e-3231" :question/problem "Find the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y = 0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept occurs at the point $(-3,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/62/6242b2af154ffea71d523bea97cb44888077b1634f6dd3d2ee05da4feb8e3f5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "905861f1-14b5-59f8-980e-6bd962740620" :question/math-academy-id "e-3232" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 9+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-1)^2 \\geq 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y-1)^2 = -5.$ Therefore, the circle has no $y$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2c8fe8a8d7b0d8946fa5c7e1122df40d16175e0bc48353f53ac30487e3dea85e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "31c41251-e19b-5933-b5f5-6cee31f7f633" :question/math-academy-id "e-3233" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercepts of the circle $x^2+y^2+2x-2y= 8,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/8e/8eb9a6488191e8cf8a5b6107f3501e7d01938cf071e77b75890d570fc99cbf7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =8 \\\\ (0)^{2}+{y}^{2}+2(0)-2y & =8 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =8+1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at  $(0,-2)$ and $(0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d31dc8e-0c35-5fd0-abcd-c7628cdd0a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433782} {:answer/id #uuid "11293f8e-d2f0-55f3-9e5f-9ac836a5188b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{2},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433783} {:answer/id #uuid "97ea5387-37c5-52b8-8290-aadaa4e30264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433798} {:answer/id #uuid "c68ed067-a5a1-5fbb-a232-736d2bd95ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433803} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ce6827-b377-535f-a602-e31fd5957524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c68ed067-a5a1-5fbb-a232-736d2bd95ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433803} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83768a9-6631-50c8-9658-6b75b2051928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85a89c6d-abe9-53d6-8315-1fd338cd6cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160269" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=2$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(0+2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+4 & =2 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+2)^{2}=-2.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e57f57f3e0cff36d9cc89a8889fa2f7fa5f4e8f9ceb5703c2d16f4ea16f38174.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40b43e26-3bfb-5ad1-97a4-8c14ea7ab819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433788} {:answer/id #uuid "88ca8278-56f5-5e3c-bfdf-6eec82b35bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(0,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433793} {:answer/id #uuid "923eb41e-5bfd-569b-a4cd-7065f6f0f080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(0,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433797} {:answer/id #uuid "ad522c1d-8789-574e-8181-f8a71fe2ee0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433799} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaee0b5-3c30-5bb2-af72-bec250543bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(0,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcaee0b5-3c30-5bb2-af72-bec250543bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(0,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433805} :answer-field/id #uuid "28942b24-6b63-5bae-98c0-24be81b19e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "727fd4af-87ee-5f68-9fac-4f3bb9ee42b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-54224" :question/problem "![](images/79/793009824d6d5a75f934fa3cfb60dd7e1b05b2918c6fef605777221bae3898af.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y=-5,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y & =-5 \\\\ (0)^{2}+{y}^{2}+4(0)-6y & =-5 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-6y+9 & =-5+9 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =3\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,1)$ and $(0,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2320e7f1-6a2e-5cac-82f0-58db10b4f2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433785} {:answer/id #uuid "3823a57d-2e1d-5238-8ca2-dc51024a5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433787} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d5c52d-52b8-5166-bd94-8dfa858f34ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-25,0)$ and $(25,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433804} {:answer/id #uuid "f17e7c42-6a58-58ca-8c98-85e3ba3734c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433809} {:answer/id #uuid "f33e6e4d-5bf4-5388-8194-4ab24fe8d402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33e6e4d-5bf4-5388-8194-4ab24fe8d402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433811} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b32cc3b-6e06-50f6-9243-7fd14609227d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9985268c-7921-5895-ae59-048de6962883" :question/math-academy-id "q-7536" :question/problem "![](images/29/299ca5fbab1351ab47dc2be6adac47b3ba3daf747e54c9b41042076cd2fee669.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{5},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22988273-1e27-5f75-910a-b7e682e7ba27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\sqrt{10})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{10})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433784} {:answer/id #uuid "44ea5368-7419-5357-8d4c-960daf0aea88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433789} {:answer/id #uuid "44f524b7-2c29-504e-97c3-2bf871a01466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433790} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5b67e1-c2c7-5027-90a7-600a95e7f72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433801} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d85322-d824-50ae-9040-be749e7db932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22988273-1e27-5f75-910a-b7e682e7ba27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\sqrt{10})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{10})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433784} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aca58f1-2210-5605-872c-de9a5f9d1e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce9524f3-b3f6-5df0-a247-5d8e5bf9f0d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7542" :question/problem "![](images/93/938eab916bca20cff978e937cbcdf02391ce9c840b996dae969fd3b616b83294.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =10 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-\\sqrt{10})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{10}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6027597a-7b05-59d1-bbd7-05bf97b739c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{2},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433791} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6e9076-4fad-5afe-b63f-c3d17654fc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2-\\sqrt{7},0)$ and $(2+\\sqr